          %p Specifically, consider the following pro-set, which is indexed on the natural numbers with the inverse ordering:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi i
                  %mo →
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mn 2
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \cdots \to S_i \to \cdots \to S_2 \to S_1 \to S_0
          %p
            We define
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi i
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mo ×
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ∼
                        %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_i = (\mathbb{N} \times \{a,b\}) / {\sim_i}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∼
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sim_i
            is the equivalence relation generated by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
